# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# READ THIS BEFORE EDITING.
#
# The Vercel CLI uses this file INSTEAD OF `.gitignore`, not in addition to it.
# The moment this file exists, every rule in `.gitignore` stops applying to the
# upload — so anything that must not leave this machine has to be repeated here.
#
# That is not a theoretical risk: the first deploy from this repo shipped the
# real `.env`, and the build log said so — "Detected .env file, it is strongly
# recommended to use Vercel's env handling instead".
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Secrets. The deployment reads its configuration from Vercel's own env store,
# which `npm run vercel:env` populates. A .env in the upload is both redundant
# and a copy of every key sitting in the build's source archive.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Build output and dependencies. Vercel installs and builds from scratch, so
# uploading these achieves nothing but a slower deploy — and a stale .next can
# confuse the build.
node_modules
.next
out
build
coverage
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Credentials and local state.
*.pem
.vercel

# Internal planning notes and agent instructions. `next build` never reads them
# and there is no reason to hand them to a third party along with the source.
ADMIN-UI.md
ARCHITECTURE.md
PLAN-BACKEND-FACULTY-ADMIN.md
AGENTS.md
CLAUDE.md
.claude/
docs/

# Local tooling. `vercel:env` runs from a laptop, never from a build.
scripts/

# Noise.
.DS_Store
